<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Normans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Normands, Nortmanni, Normanni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Normans were a people of the medieval Duchy of Normandy who arose from the intermingling of Norse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlers with the inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>. Their settlement was legitimized when the Viking leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> swore fealty to King Charles III after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> in 911, and the settlers adopted the language, religion and customs of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Under their duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> they conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066, while Norman adventurers took southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and helped found the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under Roger II; Bohemond I founded the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. In 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> seized mainland Normandy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Falls were real too: after 1066 the old English nobility lost almost all its land to the <b>Normans</b>, and many free English peasants sank into serfdom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Normans</b> under Humphrey of Hauteville, Count of Apulia, defeated a Swabian-Italian-Lombard army organised by Pope Leo IX in southern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-18">18 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Byzantine–Norman wars, the <b>Normans</b> of southern Italy under Robert Guiscard, Duke of Apulia and Calabria, defeated the army of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos outside Dyrrhachium, the major Byzantine stronghold in the western Balkans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
